WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:15.878
Unit three: Sports News. Today you will write match reports with the past simple and present perfect.

4
00:00:16.178 --> 00:00:18.947
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.086
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:24.742
Match.

7
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.609
Match means a game between two teams or players.

8
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:30.845
The match ended in a draw.

9
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.696
Your turn. Say: Match.

10
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:37.476
Match.

11
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:39.102
Score.

12
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:42.859
Score means the number of points or goals in a game.

13
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.380
The final score was three to two.

14
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:48.520
Your turn. Say: Score.

15
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:52.286
Score.

16
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.098
Championship.

17
00:00:55.083 --> 00:00:58.940
Championship means a competition to find the best team or player.

18
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.223
Cambodia hosted the championship last year.

19
00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:05.957
Your turn. Say: Championship.

20
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.045
Championship.

21
00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:11.645
Athlete.

22
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.242
Athlete means a person who is trained in a sport.

23
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.093
The young athlete trains every morning.

24
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:21.103
Your turn. Say: Athlete.

25
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:24.933
Athlete.

26
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:26.464
Tournament.

27
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.947
Tournament means a series of games in which players compete to win.

28
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.677
Sixteen teams entered the tournament.

29
00:01:34.283 --> 00:01:36.831
Your turn. Say: Tournament.

30
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:40.540
Tournament.

31
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:42.145
Victory.

32
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.985
Victory means success in winning a game or battle.

33
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:48.769
The victory delighted the home crowd.

34
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:51.629
Your turn. Say: Victory.

35
00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:55.328
Victory.

36
00:01:56.083 --> 00:01:56.992
Defeat.

37
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.575
Defeat means the experience of losing a game.

38
00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:03.229
Defeat taught the team an important lesson.

39
00:02:03.817 --> 00:02:06.434
Your turn. Say: Defeat.

40
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.057
Defeat.

41
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:11.911
Commentary.

42
00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:16.646
Commentary means a spoken description of a game as it happens.

43
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.039
The radio commentary made the match exciting.

44
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.558
Your turn. Say: Commentary.

45
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.482
Commentary.

46
00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:31.790
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:38.518
Visal, the report says 'The home side scored twice and won the match.' Why is it in the past?

48
00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:47.154
A match report describes a finished event, so we use the past simple: scored, won, lost.

49
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.798
But I also read 'The team has reached the final.'

50
00:02:51.883 --> 00:03:02.465
That is the present perfect. It links a past result to the present situation, so it is common in sports headlines and live commentary.

51
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.467
So the score and the commentary use different tenses?

52
00:03:07.583 --> 00:03:15.019
Often yes. The report gives the past facts, while commentary and updates show what has happened so far.

53
00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:17.679
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:18.517 --> 00:03:22.827
Grammar focus: Past simple vs present perfect in sports news.

55
00:03:23.127 --> 00:03:24.313
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:25.017 --> 00:03:27.643
The home side scored twice in the second half.

57
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.223
The champions lost their first match yesterday.

58
00:03:32.117 --> 00:03:34.673
The team has reached the final for the first time.

59
00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:42.663
Use the past simple with a finished time (yesterday, in the second half). Use the present perfect when the result still matters now.

60
00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:47.451
Be careful! Don't say: The team has won yesterday.

61
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.237
Say: The team won yesterday.

62
00:03:51.537 --> 00:03:54.047
A finished time needs the past simple.

63
00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:57.520
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:58.333 --> 00:03:59.741
Match Day Report.

65
00:04:00.041 --> 00:04:03.735
Thousands of fans filled the stadium for the final match of the tournament.

66
00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:08.625
The home side scored twice in the second half and secured a famous victory.

67
00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:13.444
The visiting athletes played well, but defeat came after a late goal.

68
00:04:14.433 --> 00:04:17.977
The score of two to one has given the club its first championship.

69
00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:23.079
Radio commentary carried the excitement to listeners across the country.

70
00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:28.610
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:31.856
The home side blank twice in the second half.

72
00:04:32.156 --> 00:04:35.867
A, scored. B, has scored. C, scoring.

73
00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:41.154
The answer is A: scored.

74
00:04:42.317 --> 00:04:44.560
Success in winning a game is a blank.

75
00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.339
A, defeat. B, victory. C, commentary.

76
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.772
The answer is B: victory.

77
00:04:54.917 --> 00:04:57.253
A spoken description of a game is blank.

78
00:04:57.553 --> 00:05:01.717
A, commentary. B, a byline. C, a score.

79
00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.382
The answer is A: commentary.

80
00:05:08.517 --> 00:05:11.631
Choose the best sentence about a result that still matters.

81
00:05:11.931 --> 00:05:18.487
A, The team reached yesterday the final.. B, The team has reached the final.. C, The team reach the final..

82
00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:25.217
The answer is B: The team has reached the final..

83
00:05:26.383 --> 00:05:29.242
According to the reading, the score gave the club its first…

84
00:05:29.542 --> 00:05:33.056
A, tournament. B, championship. C, match.

85
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.041
The answer is B: championship.

86
00:05:40.233 --> 00:05:43.431
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:44.283 --> 00:05:47.455
What makes sports news exciting for readers to follow?

88
00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:57.866
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:58.166 --> 00:06:20.642
Sports news is exciting because the result is never certain. Readers feel the hope of victory and the pain of defeat together with the athletes. Fast scores and live commentary also create a shared experience, so fans can talk about every match. Finally, many sports stories show courage and hard work.

90
00:06:20.942 --> 00:06:22.710
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:23.617 --> 00:06:29.322
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past simple vs present perfect in sports news.

92
00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:34.086
Today's value is sportsmanship. Play fair and shake hands, win or lose.

93
00:06:34.386 --> 00:06:36.934
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:06:37.234 --> 00:06:39.956
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

